  change log trivially easy — one insert in the same transaction as the task write, which is exactly the property that
  makes layers 3–5 cheap.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What stack should this be built on? → One server + SQLite + SSE (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last clarifying question before I put a design together. The task model's shape is really determined by what's worth
  being notified about — a field nobody gets notified on is just a field, but every notifiable field adds an event type,
  a fan-out rule, and a line in the inbox UI.

  [1mThe fork:[0m each capability below is roughly one event type and one subscription rule. Assignment and comments are the
  two that make a notification system actually earn its place on a small team — they're inherently directed at a
  specific person. Due dates are the one that quietly pulls in a scheduler, since nothing [3mhappens[0m at a due date unless
  something is polling for it; that's real added infrastructure, not just a column.

  Pick what the task core needs. I'll YAGNI anything you leave out, and adding a field later is cheap — adding the
  scheduler later is the only one with real cost.

